d and faster, and can combine with \"All Simples\" (2) and \"All Pungs\" (6) = 8 points exactly, which is enough to win. I also have exposed kong, so \"All Pungs\" requires four pungs/kongs total \u2013 I have one kong already, so need three more pungs. I have three pairs, so if I can turn each into a pung, that's three, total four. Perfect. So I should try to pung W4, W7, B1. For that, I need to draw the third tile for each. Meanwhile, I should discard tiles that are not helping this goal.\n\nSo I should keep: W4 (pair), W7 (pair), B1 (pair). And discard other singles: W2, W5, W8, T6, B4. Which to discard first? I need to consider safety and tile availability. W5 has been discarded before (previous played W5), so might be safe. W2 has been played by next and previous? Next played W2, previous played W2. So W2 is relatively safe. W8 played by previous and across? Across played W8, previous played W8. So also safe. T6 played by next (Play T6 twice?) History: next played T6, Play T6. So T6 is out. B4 played by across (Play B4). So all are relatively safe.\n\nBut I also need to consider if discarding a tile might give someone a chow, especially next player who can chi from me. Next player's meld: B3-4-5 chow. Their discards include W4, W2, T6, etc. They might be collecting W tiles? Not sure. But they can only chi if I discard a tile that completes a sequence with their hand, and only from my left (previous player is to my left? Wait: \"previous player\" is the one before me in turn order? Usually, previous player is the one who just played before me, i.e., to my left. So the player who can chi from my discard is the next player? Actually, in Mahjong, the player immediately after you (to your right) can only pung or kong, not chi. Chi is only allowed from the discard of the player immediately before you (to your left). So in this context, \"previous player\" is the one who played just before me, i.e., to my left. So if I discard, the player to my right (next player) can